From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] avrt: the multimedia class scheduler's tasks, and thread ordering groups

- AvSetMmThreadCharacteristics took any name and did nothing. A thread now
  joins a task of the system profile (Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\
  CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile\Tasks\<task>: Priority,
  Scheduling Category; Windows' default tasks -- Audio, Capture,
  Distribution, Games, Playback, Pro Audio, Window Manager -- when the key
  is not there); an unknown task is ERROR_INVALID_TASK_NAME. Its thread
  priority is raised by the task's category (High: time critical; Medium:
  highest or above normal by the task's priority) and put back by
  AvRevertMmThreadCharacteristics; a task index is given when asked.
- AvSetMmThreadPriority moves the thread around the task's priority
  (critical: time critical); AvQuerySystemResponsiveness reads the
  profile's SystemResponsiveness (20% by default);
  AvSetMmMaxThreadCharacteristics takes the more important of its two
  tasks (it refused two).
- Thread ordering groups (not exported before): a parent
  (AvRtCreateThreadOrderingGroup(Ex), a new GUID when given GUID_NULL,
  a period of 0.5 ms to 0.5 s, the timeout five periods unless given or
  infinite) and clients (AvRtJoinThreadOrderingGroup, before or after the
  parent) run in turn once a period: AvRtWaitOnThreadOrderingGroup ends a
  thread's turn and waits for its next; a turn held past the timeout
  passes on, a wait as long ends with ERROR_TIMEOUT;
  AvRtLeaveThreadOrderingGroup and AvRtDeleteThreadOrderingGroup.
- avrt.h: AvRtCreateThreadOrderingGroupExW takes a wide task name.

Gate: test/mmcss-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit). Mutants
SG_MUTANT_NO_MMCSS_BOOST, SG_MUTANT_NO_ORDERING (avrt/main.c).
